HETTIE’s cottage. A fire, a kettle, a fiddle on the wall. HETTIE mends a lantern. MUSICIAN 1 sits in the corner, tuning.

HETTIE
(singing to herself as she works)
~ SONG "Lanterns" [HETTIE]
~ Light one for the boat that’s late,
~ Light one for the gull,
~ Light one for the man who swore
~ The sea was never full.
~
~ Lanterns on the water,
~ Lanterns on the hill,
~ Somebody’s coming home tonight
~ And somebody always will.
~ END SONG

A knock. CORNELIUS enters, old and bent, and takes the fiddle off the wall without asking.

CORNELIUS
That’s the old tune. Your mother used to sing it worse.

HETTIE
Cornelius. You’re supposed to be in bed.

CORNELIUS
I’m supposed to be dead, according to the doctor. I’ve outlived three of him.

MUSICIAN 1
(offering a note)
A?

CORNELIUS
A will do. Everything starts on A, if you’re not fussy.

CORNELIUS
(he plays, then sings)
~ SONG
~ Play me out on the ebbing tide,
~ Play me past the bar,
~ Tell them all I went out wide
~ And didn’t go too far.
~ END SONG

HETTIE
That’s not a lament. That’s a complaint.

CORNELIUS
Same thing, played slower.
